dict(img=None,
stem="Arrhythmias arise from abnormal automaticity, triggered activity, or re-entry.{FIG}Which mechanism is the most common, especially in SVT?",
choices=["Delayed afterdepolarizations","Early afterdepolarizations","Enhanced automaticity","Parasystole","Re-entry"], key=4,
exp="The three mechanisms:<br>• <b>Automaticity</b>: a focus fires too fast or where it shouldn't (atrial tachycardia, junctional ectopic tachycardia).<br>• <b>Triggered activity</b>: EADs (long QT → torsades) or DADs (digoxin, ischemic VT/VF).<br>• <b>Re-entry</b>: a <b>short circuit between two pathways</b> with different conduction and refractory properties. It is the <b>most common</b>, especially in SVT: AVNRT, AVRT, flutter and scar VT.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Re-entry, a circuit between two pathways with different properties, is the most common arrhythmia mechanism, especially in SVT.",
wrong={0:"DADs are triggered activity, classically from digoxin toxicity or ischemia, and they are less common.",
1:"EADs cause torsades in long QT, and they are not the main SVT mechanism.",
2:"Automaticity explains focal atrial and junctional tachycardias, not most SVTs.",
3:"Parasystole is a rare independent ectopic focus."},
eli5="Most fast rhythms are caused by an electrical signal getting stuck going round and round a loop, called re-entry. It's like a car stuck in a traffic circle."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 27-year-old woman has a regular narrow-complex tachycardia at 190/min with no visible P waves. The diagnosis is typical AV nodal re-entrant tachycardia.{FIG}Why are the P waves not visible?",
choices=["The atria are not depolarizing","Atria and ventricles fire together","The P waves are too small to record","The P waves fall after every T wave","There is complete AV block"], key=1,
exp="In typical <b>AVNRT</b>, the impulse goes <b>down the slow pathway</b> and <b>up the fast pathway</b> inside the AV node. So the <b>atria (activated backward) and the ventricles fire at the same time</b>, and the P wave is <b>buried inside the QRS</b>. A narrow, regular tachycardia with no visible P waves is a very common tip-off for AVNRT.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> In typical AVNRT the atria and ventricles depolarize simultaneously, so the P wave is hidden in the QRS.",
wrong={0:"The atria are depolarizing, backward, at the same instant as the ventricles.",
2:"P waves are normally visible. Their timing hides them here.",
3:"A P wave after the T is the pattern of some other SVTs, not typical AVNRT.",
4:"Complete block would dissociate P waves and QRS complexes, and the re-entry circuit needs conduction."},
eli5="The signal loops inside the AV node and fires the upper and lower chambers at the exact same moment. The small P wave hides inside the big QRS spike."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A stable patient with a regular narrow-complex SVT is given a rapid IV push of adenosine 6 mg followed by a saline flush.{FIG}Where does adenosine mainly act to stop this arrhythmia?",
choices=["Accessory pathway","AV node","Pulmonary veins","SA node","Ventricular myocardium"], key=1,
exp="<b>Adenosine</b> acts mainly at the <b>AV node</b>, briefly slowing or blocking conduction so that re-entry circuits that use the node (AVNRT, AVRT) break. Dose: <b>6 mg rapid push + flush</b>, then 12 mg. Its half-life is <b>under 5 seconds</b>, so it doesn't accumulate. Side effects: flushing, chest pain, hypotension, and a few seconds of asystole. Avoid it in heart transplant recipients (prolonged asystole) and WPW (it can trigger AF → VF).<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Adenosine terminates AV-node-dependent SVT by transiently blocking AV nodal conduction. Its half-life is under 5 seconds.",
wrong={0:"Adenosine doesn't block the accessory pathway, which is why it's dangerous in WPW.",
2:"The pulmonary veins are the source of AF triggers.",
3:"Adenosine can slow the SA node, but its therapeutic target is the AV node.",
4:"Adenosine has little effect on ventricular muscle."},
eli5="Adenosine briefly shuts the AV node, the gate between upper and lower chambers. If the fast rhythm needs that gate to loop, closing it for a moment breaks the loop."),

dict(img=None,
stem="Carotid sinus massage is attempted in a stable patient with a narrow regular tachycardia.{FIG}Through which of the following mechanisms does carotid sinus massage terminate AV nodal re-entry?",
choices=["Blocking β1 receptors on the SA node","Increasing vagal tone at the AV node level","Releasing adenosine from the carotid body","Stimulating carotid body chemoreceptors","Stimulating sympathetic outflow to the ventricles"], key=1,
exp="Pressure on the <b>carotid sinus</b> stimulates its <b>baroreceptors</b> (glossopharyngeal nerve, CN IX afferent) → the brainstem responds with <b>vagal (parasympathetic) outflow</b> → slower AV-node conduction → re-entry through the node can break. \"Patients think you're a wizard, but it's just physiology.\" Note that it's the carotid <b>sinus</b>, not the carotid body (a chemoreceptor); the recording's slip. Listen for bruits first, and don't do it after a recent stroke or TIA.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Carotid sinus massage stimulates baroreceptors and increases vagal tone, slowing AV-node conduction to terminate AVNRT.",
wrong={0:"Massage doesn't block β-receptors. It increases vagal tone.",
2:"The carotid body doesn't release adenosine.",
3:"The carotid body is a <b>chemoreceptor</b> (O₂, CO₂, pH), not the target of massage.",
4:"Massage <b>reduces</b> sympathetic and increases vagal tone."},
eli5="Pressing on the carotid sinus fools the body into thinking blood pressure is high. The brain responds by turning up the vagus nerve, which slows the AV node and can stop the racing rhythm."),

